PIMID: the guest's standard streams are never a terminal (-stdio-notty)

QEMU user mode passes the guest's standard input, output and error through to
the host's. When one of them was a terminal, the guest's C library saw a
character device that answers the terminal ioctls: it line-buffered the stream
(glibc's _IO_file_doallocate tests the device and isatty) and took other
branches than for a file or a pipe, so a run started at a terminal did other
work before and around its writes than the same run with its output in a log
file, and multi-threaded and message-passing results differed.

With -stdio-notty (or QEMU_STDIO_NOTTY) the guest's fds 0, 1 and 2 are never a
terminal: terminal ioctls on them (type 'T', TCGETS and the rest) fail with
ENOTTY, and fstat, newfstatat with AT_EMPTY_PATH and statx report a pipe
(S_IFIFO, mode 0600, size 0) owned by the process's own user and group, with
device, inode and times 0. The host stream's owner, device, inode and times
are not passed through: the guest's C library reads only the type and block
size, but the rest stays in the guest's memory (the stat buffer on the
stack), so a terminal (group tty) or a log file of another group changed the
data a later read of that memory carried. Without the option QEMU is
unchanged.

